\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{On the predictability of phase-modulated severity.}
One might argue that the qualitative direction---importance-based eviction degrades when attention distributes broadly---is intuitive.
The surprise lies in the quantitative structure: the severity gradient ($69$--$274{\times}$; Table~\ref{tab:main-results}) far exceeds a naive ``somewhat worse'' prediction, compound interaction concentrates in low-sparsity phases while high-sparsity phases exhibit near-zero interaction ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\text{high}} < 0.06$), and a crossover emerges where importance-based eviction simultaneously outperforms and underperforms random depending on phase---a reversal that cannot be derived from entropy arguments alone. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030

\paragraph{Random masking as falsification.}
If compound severity amplification were inherent to joint compression, random eviction should exhibit a comparable phase gradient.
It does not: 7/8 conditions across both models show no significant phase modulation; the single exception (Qwen $s{=}0.30$, $p{=}0.012$) has negligible effect size ($d_z{=}{-}0.04$), isolating the amplification as strategy-specific. % exp_id: bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030, bf16_qw_rand_s015..s030

\paragraph{Cross-model replication.}
The phase-strategy interaction replicates with perfect ordinal agreement (Spearman $\rho{=}1.0$, gradient permutation $p{=}0.043$), though Qwen's absolute magnitude is approximately one-fifth of DeepSeek's with a higher onset threshold ($s \geq 0.20$ vs.\ $s{=}0.15$).
We conjecture that reasoning distillation amplifies the interaction by producing sharper internal phase transitions. % exp_id: cross_model_correlation

\paragraph{Implications for deployment.}
Uniform compression budgets are suboptimal for reasoning models: compound severity concentrates in low-sparsity phases where discrimination collapse renders importance-based eviction unreliable.
A \emph{phase-aware compression budget}---allocating lower sparsity to low-sparsity phases and reclaiming capacity in high-sparsity phases---could mitigate severity amplification while preserving compression gains.
The severity gradient $G$ (\S\ref{sec:phase-amplification}) provides a natural signal for such allocation.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
A phase-gated controller that monitors attention sparsity in real time and dynamically adjusts compound sparsity budgets per phase is the natural next step.
Completing the mechanistic picture---particularly the model-dependent entropy component (\S\ref{sec:discrimination-collapse})---and scaling to larger models and diverse reasoning tasks would establish generality.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We showed that compound sparsity severity is not a fixed property of the sparsity level but an emergent phase-strategy interaction spanning over an order of magnitude within a single generation. Under importance-based eviction, the median II difference between low- and high-sparsity phases increases monotonically from $0.39$ to $14.59$ ($d_z$: $0.44 \to 0.80$; all $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$), while random eviction produces no significant phase modulation in 7/8 conditions (C1, C2). At $s{=}0.25$, a crossover emerges where importance-based eviction simultaneously outperforms and underperforms random depending on phase (C3). The pattern replicates on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct at $s \geq 0.20$ with attenuated magnitude (C5), and the severity gradient grows sub-exponentially (C6). Discrimination collapse---whereby head pruning degrades importance-score discriminability---provides a robust mechanistic explanation confirmed across both models (C4). These findings challenge the phase-invariance assumption and motivate phase-aware compression budgets for reasoning models.

\section*{Limitations}

Both models share the Qwen2 architecture; cross-architecture validation (e.g., LLaMA, Mistral) remains future work. All experiments are at the 7B scale; whether severity gradients persist at larger scales is untested. Evaluation is limited to MATH-500; different reasoning domains may induce different phase structures. The discrimination collapse mechanism is supported by correlational evidence but lacks direct causal validation. While discrimination collapse (Gini/CV reduction) is consistent across all conditions, the entropy redistribution component replicates on only 2/4 sparsity levels for Qwen, suggesting model-dependent modulation.

